<header><p class="st-kicker">city state · Mesoamerica and the Caribbean</p><h1 class="st-title">Bonampak</h1><p class="st-meta">600 CE – 800 CE (approx.) · capital Bonampak</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A small Maya city in the Lacandon forest whose murals give the most vivid surviving picture of a Classic Maya court, battle and ceremony.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Bonampak, anciently Usiij Witz, was a minor centre near the Lacanjá river in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chiapas">Chiapas</a>. Its lords are attested in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/late-classic-period">Late Classic</a> from around 600 CE and were for long periods subordinate to their more powerful neighbour <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/yaxchilan">Yaxchilan</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Structure 1 holds three painted rooms, created around 790 under the ruler <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yajaw-chan-muwaan">Yajaw Chan Muwaan</a>. They show the presentation of an heir, a fierce battle, the judgement and torture of captives, and celebrations with dancers and musicians. The murals reveal costume, weapons, music and court hierarchy in rare detail, and show the close involvement of Yaxchilan's royal house.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The murals were never finished, and Bonampak was abandoned soon after 800 CE. They were revealed to outsiders in 1946 by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lacandon-maya">Lacandon Maya</a> guides, transforming the image of the Maya as peaceful.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/bonampak.jpg" alt="Bonampak" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: Dbannasch · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Zona_arqueologica_Bonampak_01_ID_ZA30_DBannasch.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>600 CE – 800 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>city state</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>theocratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Bonampak</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Yajaw Chan Muwaan</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Maya religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Classic Maya</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Mexico</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Usiij Witz</dd></dl></aside></div>

